    "blurb": "The show delivers a blistering Dancin' In The Street and an expansive Viola Lee Blues, both anchored by tight rhythm work. Jerry’s snaky solo shines while Pigpen’s organ sits unusually forward, adding weight to the jams. Soundboard capture is crystal‑clear, making this performance a must‑listen.",
    "review": "On April 12, 1970 at the Fillmore West the Dead served up one of their early‑’70 firestorms. The set bursts open with a snarling Dancin' In The Street where Jerry Garcia’s solo slithers and blazes, driving the band into a scorching groove that never lets up. Phil Lesh lays down a solid, pounding bass foundation while the dual drummers lock in tight, highlighted by a crisp drum break that adds extra punch. Pigpen’s organ is unusually forward in the mix, giving an extra heft to the soaring Viola Lee Blues and the seamless Candyman‑to‑Viola transition. The soundboard recording captures this energy with crystal‑clear balance; only minor three‑second gaps interrupt the flow, but they do little to diminish the overall listening experience.",
